===Verb===
[[File:Tobogganing at University of Wisconsin, Madison, 1929.jpg|thumb|Tobogganing, on a very long toboggan]]
{{en-verb|pres_ptc2=tobogganning|past2=tobogganned}}

# To slide down a hill on a [[toboggan#noun|toboggan]] or other object.
#* {{quote-book|en|1887|E. Katherine (Emily Katherine) Bates|A Year in the Great Republic|text=Mr. Macaulay, the landlord, insisted upon trying to "'''toboggan'''" us down the mountain on the saddle cloth of one of the horses, an attempt that ended of course in disaster, for the surface was much too small for the three of us, and the snow too soft for the purpose.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|1888|Alfred Thomas Tucker Wise|Alpine winter in its medical aspects|text=The aspect of this patient was greatly changed for the better; she was able to skate, '''toboggan''', and mount 500 feet of Maloja Pass without fatigue.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|1916|William John Thomas, (John) Doran, Henry Frederick Turle, Joseph Knight, Vernon Horace Rendall, Florence Hayllar|Notes and Queries''|text=I froze my toes some years ago, while '''tobogganing''', and was unaware of it until I took off my shoe and walked across the room, when the unusual noise on the boards attracted my attention.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|2006|Nita Hughes|The Cathar Legacy|text=The hillside, lined with a coating of wet leaves ready to '''toboggan''' her down the slope, made her grateful for a few saplings that provided handholds.}}
# {{lb|en|figurative}} To [[go downhill]] unstoppably until one reaches the bottom.
#* '''1945''', US House of Representatives, ''1945 extension of the Reciprocal trade agreements act: hearings before the Committee on finance, United States Senate, Seventy-ninth Congress, first session, on H.R. 3240, an act to extend the authority of the President under section 350 of the Tariff act of 1930, as amended, and for other purposes'':
#*: {{quote|en|A depression in one nation can become the slide on which our civilization would '''toboggan''' into economic collapse.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|2006|Keith Dixon|Altered Life|text=I can't win, can I? You think I'm posh and my folks think I'm '''tobogganing''' down-market faster than the royal family.}}
# {{lb|en|aviation|intransitive}} To [[fly]] sharply [[downward]] so as to build up [[speed]] to facilitate [[in-flight]] [[refueling]] of a faster aircraft.
#* {{quote-journal|en|1961|||Wings|volume=31-33|page=11|text=Aircraft returning to refuel at about three o'clock have '''tobogganed''' down to their spats in the mud whilst early in the morning one could bounce a crowbar on the frozen ground.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|2005|||title=The Mobility Forum: The Journal of the Air Mobility Command|page=21|text=How did a piston engine tanker refuel a faster jet bomber? It "'''tobogganed'''" - the refueling connection would be made high up and then the bomber and tanker flew "downhill" together enabling the tanker to pick up more speed.}}

